<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaghri Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/989">989</a>–<a href="/years/1060">1060</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Chaghri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaghri Beg was a grandson of Seljuk and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a>, with whom he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> in the early eleventh century. The brothers captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and defeated the Ghaznavids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a> in 1040. The victory secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> to the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg.jpg" alt="Chaghri Beg" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ahmad_Qavurt,_minted_in_Jiroft.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chaghri and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> were the sons of Mikail and the grandsons of Seljuk, the Turkic clan leader after whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuk Empire</a> was named. Early in the 11th century the family left its former home and settled near the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jand">Jend</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, where it accepted the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> of Transoxania. After the Karakhanids were defeated by the Ghaznavids, the Seljuks were able to gain independence. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-warlord">Seljuk Beg</a> died in 1009, Tughril and Chaghri took over the administration of Jand, but frequent clashes with the ruler of Yengikent led them to abandon it in about 1016.</p><p>Very little is known of the brothers' lives until 1025. Both were raised by their grandfather until they were fifteen, and they fought with the Karakhanid noble Ali Tigin against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1025 Mahmud lured and captured Arslan Isra'il, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-warlord">Seljuk Beg</a>, and scattered the Seljuk camps across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. Mahmud then allowed about four thousand Seljuk families to settle near Sarakhs, Farawa and Abiward as frontier guards, but they harassed the local people, and he defeated them at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribat</a>-i-Farawa in 1028. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> and Chaghri refused to recognise the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-tegin">Ali Tegin</a> and began to claim power over the Seljuk tribes.</p><p>From 1035 to 1037 the brothers fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masud-i-of-ghazni">Mas'ud I of Ghazni</a>, and Chaghri captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. Between 1038 and 1040 he fought the Ghaznavids, usually by hit and run, until the major clash at Dandanaqan. Tughril was hesitant and preferred to continue the raids, but Chaghri commanded the army and favoured a direct fight, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> defeated a numerically superior Ghaznavid force. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> held after the battle divided the empire. Tughril ruled the west, and Chaghri ruled eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, collectively called Greater Khorasan. He later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 1048 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in 1056, and in 1056 married his daughter Khadija Arslan Khatun to the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaim">Al-Qa'im</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chaghri died in Sarakhs, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Earlier Turkic tradition let brothers share in government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a>, Chaghri and other family members all took part in founding the empire. Tughril took the title sultan, but it was Chaghri's sons who continued it. He had six sons and four daughters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> became sultan in 1064, and all the later members of the Great Seljuk line descended from Chaghri. Kavurt became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, which later became fully independent, and Yaquti governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Chaghri's daughters made marriages into the Buyid, Kakuyid and Uqaylid houses as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chaghri Beg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1030s Turkmen tribes led by the Seljuk brothers Tughril and <b>Chaghri</b> crossed the Oxus into Khurasan, where the taxation of Mas'ud's officials had alienated the towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Seljuk's grandsons Tughril and <b>Chaghri</b> led their tribesmen into Khorasan in the 1030s, taking Merv and Nishapur in 1037–1038 and throwing off Ghaznavid authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Sarakhs, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.55,61.16&amp;year=1035" aria-label="Sarakhs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Chaghri Beg"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5080 4441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5080 4441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.55,61.16&amp;year=1035">What was here in 1035? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
